  <prompt>
    Implement FR-24 (import round-trip — existing revisions). On Load, run the EXISTING
    `DocxAnnotationReader.Read` (`Services/Compose/DocxAnnotationReader.cs:68` — it already reads
    ALL native `w:ins`/`w:del` regardless of authorship; see class doc `DocxAnnotationReader.cs:14-16`
    + `RecoveredRevision` record `DocxAnnotationReader.cs:327-348`) server-side alongside the mammoth
    convert, project the resulting `RecoveredRevision` list onto the Load response, and render each as
    a **first-class tracked change** (insertion/deletion mark) in the editor mount — NOT flattened by
    mammoth to prose. This is **WIRING, not building** (design §7 — "the reader is not the gap, the
    editor mount is"): the reader is done; the gap is the Load-response projection + the in-editor
    render. Extends `ComposeService.LoadAsync` (`ComposeService.cs:168`). The imported revisions must be
    accept/reject-able like any R2 redline mark, and must anchor by `w14:paraId` (E2) so task 052 can
    prove they survive save. Do NOT re-derive or reimplement the reader — reuse it verbatim.
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Senior full-stack developer fluent in the Microsoft Open XML SDK 3.x read path, the Spaarke
    Compose BFF (`ComposeService.LoadAsync` load orchestration), the TipTap MIT-base editor with the
    R2 home-grown `insertion`/`deletion` marks, and the mirror-first contract discipline
    (`Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/types` ↔ `Services/Compose/*.cs`). Comfortable wiring an existing
    server-side reader's output into a client render without touching the reader itself.
  </role>

  <goal>
    `ComposeService.LoadAsync` runs the existing `DocxAnnotationReader.Read` on the load-time `.docx`
    bytes and projects `RecoveredRevision` (kind/author/date/text/anchorText/paragraphHint, plus the
    E2 `paraId`) onto the Load response contract (additive, mirror-first). The client editor mount
    renders each recovered `w:ins`/`w:del` as a first-class TipTap insertion/deletion mark — visible,
    author/date-attributed, and accept/reject-able — instead of letting mammoth flatten it to prose.
    Imported revisions anchor by `paraId` (primary) with the `AnnotationReanchorService` fuzzy fallback
    retained. A through-the-wire slice assertion (full authorship in task 052) confirms a Word-redlined
    doc opens with its revisions visible and actionable. Matching unit tests in
    `tests/unit/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests/Services/Compose/` cover the Load-projection.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      Import round-trip is in R3 core (D2) as a parallel track behind the E1/E2 fidelity core, on which
      it depends for the paraId substrate (E2) and the retained-original save (E1). Design §7 is the
      controlling section: the existing-mark READER already ships (`DocxAnnotationReader.cs:14-16,327-348`
      — built to recover human Word-for-Web edits, returns `RecoveredRevision` with
      author/date/anchorText/paragraphHint), but the editor mount path uses mammoth
      (`docxBridge.ts:77-86`), which flattens `w:ins`/`w:del` to prose before the editor sees them. So
      FR-24 is the Load-response projection + in-editor render of `RecoveredRevision`, attached by
      `paraId` (E2) so it survives the retained-original save (E1). Design §2 records the reader as an
      existing asset ("The reader is not the gap — the editor mount is").
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Compose/ComposeService.cs — extend `LoadAsync` (`:168`) to run `DocxAnnotationReader.Read` on the load-time bytes and project `RecoveredRevision` onto the Load response (design §2 existing-mark reader row, §7, §9 Load row)</file>
      <file role="canonical-reuse">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Compose/DocxAnnotationReader.cs — REUSE VERBATIM: `Read(byte[])` (`:68`) already reads all `w:ins`/`w:del` regardless of authorship (`:14-16`); `RecoveredRevision` record (`:327-348`). Do NOT modify or reimplement.</file>
      <file role="modify">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/types/compose-contracts.ts — additive Load-response field carrying imported revisions; `AnchoredAnnotationAnchor` (`:108-115`) already gains `paraId` in E2 (task 012/030) — reuse it</file>
      <file role="modify">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeEditor.tsx — render recovered revisions as first-class `insertion`/`deletion` marks on mount (accept/reject-able), instead of mammoth-flattened prose</file>
      <file role="reference">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/utils/docxBridge.ts — the mammoth mount path (`:77-86`) that currently flattens marks; the imported revisions bypass the flatten by riding the Load-response projection</file>
      <file role="design">projects/spaarkeai-compose-r3/design.md §7 (import round-trip — reader exists, mount is the gap), §2 (existing-mark reader row), §10 (component reuse map — import parser row), §8 (LOAD pipeline)</file>
    </relevant-files>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency task="010" status="pending">FR-08 server pre-parse + `w14:paraId` minting on Load — imported revisions attach by paraId; the paraId map must exist on the Load response first.</dependency>
      <dependency task="012" status="pending">FR-11/FR-12 paraId-primary anchoring + fuzzy fallback — imported revisions anchor by paraId first, fuzzy fallback second.</dependency>
      <dependency task="022" status="pending">FR-01 E1 cutover (retained-original baseline) — the substrate imported marks must survive across a dirty save (proven in task 052).</dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project / design §7">This is WIRING, not building. REUSE `DocxAnnotationReader` verbatim — do NOT modify, fork, or reimplement the reader. The only new code is the Load-response projection + the in-editor render.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-007">NO `Microsoft.Graph` types above the `SpeFileStore`/Infrastructure facade. The reader operates on `byte[]`; `LoadAsync` already obtains bytes via the facade — do not introduce a Graph type in `Services/Compose/`.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-013 / NFR-05">`Services/Compose/` MUST NOT inject `IOpenAiClient`/executor/routing types. Import projection is a deterministic OOXML-read + render concern with zero AI reach. Tier-1 NetArchTest stays green.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project / MUST anchor by paraId">Imported revisions MUST anchor primarily by `w14:paraId` (E2); the `AnnotationReanchorService` fuzzy match is retained as the cross-Word-session fallback (Word regenerates paraIds on external edits).</constraint>
    <constraint source="NFR-03 / owner rule">No TipTap product feature (paid or unpaid). The imported revisions render via the R2 home-grown `insertion`/`deletion` marks on the MIT base — no `@tiptap-pro/*`, no TipTap TrackChanges module.</constraint>
    <constraint source="NFR-10 / ADR-021 / ADR-028">New UI is Fluent v9 + dark mode; client fetches use `@spaarke/auth`.</constraint>
    <constraint source="NFR-01 / §10 BFF hygiene / ADR-029">Report BFF publish-size absolute + delta vs the ~49.63 MB baseline (the reader is already referenced → expect ~0 MB delta; state it). No new NuGet. Verify no new HIGH CVE.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project / §10 Placement Justification">State the Placement Justification in the PR description (Load-projection lives in `Services/Compose/`, reusing the existing reader + LoadAsync seam — no new server surface), citing bff-extensions.md.</constraint>
    <constraint source="NFR-06 / ADR-038">The through-the-wire seam slice test for imported-marks survival is authored in task 052; this task adds Load-projection unit tests at the KEEP path. No `Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;`, no DI-registration/ctor-null tests.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>Open XML revision read; ComposeService Load orchestration; TipTap insertion/deletion marks; paraId anchoring</topic>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md</file>
